wer(u.email)` wraps the column in a function. A plain index on `email` can't be used, so the planner evaluates `lower()` on every row. This is the main problem. The lookup should hit one row but costs O(n).\n2. **Scan on `orders`**: this happens if `orders.user_id` has no index. Once the user is found, the join has to find that user's orders. Without an index, that means a sequential scan of `orders`, or a hash join that reads all of it. Many schemas declare a foreign key without indexing it, and some engines don't create the index automatically. I haven't seen the schema, so check this.\n3. **Wider row reads from `SELECT *`**: this returns every column from both tables. It repeats the `users` columns on every order row, and it blocks index-only scans. It also breaks callers if columns are added later, and it can pull in large or sensitive columns such as blobs and password hashes.\n\n## Fixes\n\n**1. Make the email lookup indexable.** Pick one:\n\n- Expression index. This is the least invasive option and matches the query as written:\n  ```sql\n  CREATE INDEX idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));\n  ```\n  Use `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX` if emails must be unique case-insensitively. The query's expression must match the index expression exactly.\n- Case-insensitive type. In Postgres, make the column `citext` with a unique index, and then query `WHERE u.email = 'a@example.org'`. In MySQL, the default collations are already case-insensitive, so drop `lower()` and index `email`.\n- Normalize on write. Store emails lowercased, add a plain unique index, and lowercase the input in the application. This is the most portable option.\n\n**2. Index the join key:**\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_user_id ON orders (user_id);\n```\nSkip this if one already exists. With both indexes, the plan becomes an index seek on `users` followed by an index range scan on `orders`.\n\n**3. Select only the columns you need:**\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM orders o\nJOIN users u ON u.id = o.user_id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\nAdjust the column list to what the caller uses. If you only need orders, select `o.*` and use `WHERE o.user_id = (SELECT id FROM users WHERE lower(email) = ...)`. That avoids repeating user columns on every row.\n\n**4. Optional: add `ORDER BY` and `LIMIT`.** A user can have many orders. If the caller wants recent ones, add `ORDER BY o.created_at DESC LIMIT n`. Then use `(user_id, created_at DESC)` as the index on `orders` so the sort is free.\n\n## Verify\nRun `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` before and after. You want an index scan on `users` and an index or bitmap scan on `orders`, with no `Seq Scan` on either. I only had the query text, so I haven't run it, and the row counts and existing indexes are unknown to me.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}